A field component declares a bound input in ControlManifest.Input.xml. The TypeScript class compiles, but the component does not refresh when the framework passes changed values in the property bag.

Snippet

export class StatusBadge implements ComponentFramework.StandardControl<IInputs, IOutputs> {
    public init(
        context: ComponentFramework.Context<IInputs>,
        notifyOutputChanged: () => void,
        state: ComponentFramework.Dictionary,
        container: HTMLDivElement
    ): void {
        // initialization
    }
 
    public getOutputs(): IOutputs {
        return { };
    }
 
    public destroy(): void {
        // cleanup
    }
}
Which required interface method must be added?

A. init override

Explanation
The init override method is responsible for initializing the component and setting up the initial state. While this method is necessary for the component to function properly, it does not directly address the issue of the component not refreshing when the framework passes changed values in the property bag.
Your answer is correct
B. updateView method

Explanation
The updateView method is the required interface method that must be added to the TypeScript class. This method is called by the framework when the data within the component needs to be updated or refreshed based on changes in the property bag. By implementing the updateView method, the component will be able to reflect the updated values passed by the framework.
C. output schema method

Explanation
The output schema method is not directly related to the issue of the component not refreshing when the framework passes changed values in the property bag. This method is typically used to define the structure of the output data that the component will provide, rather than handling the updating or refreshing of the component based on external changes.
D. manifest parser

Explanation
The manifest parser is not a required interface method that needs to be added to the TypeScript class in this scenario. The manifest parser is responsible for parsing the control manifest file and extracting relevant information about the component, but it does not address the specific issue of the component not refreshing when the framework passes changed values in the property bag.
Overall explanation
StandardControl requires the core lifecycle methods that let a PCF component initialize, react to changed values, return outputs, and clean up. Microsoft documents updateView as the method called when values in the property bag change, including field values, datasets, dimensions, metadata, and other framework-provided values, so that is the missing required method in this control.



The manifest defines the component shape and the parameters that become available through context.parameters, but reacting to changed inputs is still an interface responsibility in the TypeScript implementation. In practice, that means a bound manifest property and a StandardControl implementation work together: the manifest declares the inputs, and updateView is where the control receives and renders the latest values.



Why the other options are incorrect:

• A. init override. init is already present in the snippet, so adding another initialization-style method would not solve the missing lifecycle behavior. Microsoft describes init as the place for initialization work and notes that changing or rendering refreshed dataset values belongs in updateView, not in init. This option is plausible because initialization is a required lifecycle concept, but it is the wrong lifecycle hook for responding to changed input values.



• C. output schema method. Returning outputs is part of the PCF lifecycle, but the documented StandardControl contract exposes getOutputs, not an “output schema method,” for normal control output handling. The snippet already includes getOutputs, so inventing another output-related member would not satisfy the missing required interface method. This distractor is attractive because outputs are real in PCF, but it mixes framework concepts that do not replace updateView.



• D. manifest parser. The manifest schema is declared in the XML manifest and consumed by the framework; component authors do not implement a separate “manifest parser” interface method as part of StandardControl. Microsoft’s manifest documentation describes XML elements such as control, code, and parameter definitions, while the control lifecycle methods are documented separately in the API reference. This option confuses manifest configuration with the runtime interface contract.

